---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

// Article for this career guide. Dates come from git via the same map the
// sitemap uses, so dateModified always equals this URL's sitemap lastmod;
// author and publisher are the Organization @id (see src/lib/techarticle.js).
const jsonLd = articleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/careers/index.astro',
  path: '/careers',
  headline: "Oil Rig Jobs & Careers — Roles, Hierarchy, Salary",
  description: "Complete guide to oil rig careers: every role from roustabout to toolpusher, salary ranges, required certifications, and the full progression path.",
  lang: 'en',
});

---
<BaseLayout
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  title="Oil Rig Jobs & Careers — Roles, Hierarchy, Salary"
  description="Complete guide to oil rig careers: every role from roustabout to toolpusher, salary ranges, required certifications, and the full progression path."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Careers', href: '/careers' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/careers"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/careers" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">03 — People</div>
    <h1>Oil Rig <span>Careers</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Rig careers run from roustabout ($38K+, no experience needed) through roughneck, derrickhand, and driller, up to toolpusher, OIM, and the operator-side company man ($300K+) — each rung adding pay, responsibility, and a specific certification gate. See every role below with real pay, timeline, and entry requirements.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Getting Started</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">How to Get a Job <span>on an Oil Rig</span></h2>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:800px;">
      <p>Every oil rig career starts the same way, regardless of which role you eventually want: as a <a href="/careers/roustabout">roustabout</a>, doing general labor with no certifications and no prior experience required. That's not a consolation-prize entry point — it's the deliberate on-ramp the whole industry uses, because it lets a new hire learn how a rig actually works before anyone hands them responsibility for pipe under tension or equipment that can hurt someone.</p>
      <p>The baseline requirements to get hired at all are the same across every entry-level role: a high school diploma or GED, being at least 18, passing a physical fitness assessment, and clearing a background check and drug screening. No college degree is required anywhere on this ladder until you reach specialized engineering or geology roles far above the floor. Drilling contractors (Nabors, Helmerich & Payne, Transocean, Noble, Patterson-UTI) hire directly for land and offshore crews, and energy staffing agencies (Airswift, Brunel, NES Fircroft) place entry-level workers and can fast-track your first placement, especially offshore.</p>
      <p>From there, progression runs roustabout → roughneck → <a href="/careers/derrickhand">derrickhand</a> → assistant driller → driller, typically taking 7–10 years to reach the console from a standing start, with real pay increases at every step. Promotions on a rig are almost entirely merit-based: reliability, a clean safety record, and certifications completed before they're strictly required for your current role are what actually move you up faster than the industry average. Offshore work pays 20–40% more than the onshore equivalent at every stage, in exchange for isolation and rotational schedules (commonly 14/14 or 28/28, see the <a href="/careers/rotation-schedules">rotation schedules guide</a>) rather than a daily commute.</p>
      <p>Each guide below covers one stage of that ladder in full depth — what the role actually involves day to day, the specific certifications it requires, realistic pay, and exactly how to get hired into it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Starting From Zero</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What "No Experience" <span>Actually Means</span></h2>
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:800px;">
      <p>"No experience required" is true, but it's also the line people trust least — it sounds like the kind of thing a job posting says and doesn't mean. On a rig it's literal: nobody is born knowing how to rig up a hoist or read a pressure gauge, and the industry's entire entry-level structure exists specifically to take someone who has never set foot on a location and turn them into a useful hand within weeks, not months. What follows is what that actually looks like in practice, not the recruiting-page version.</p>

      <h3>What Day One Actually Looks Like</h3>
      <p>You are not handed pipe on your first day, and nobody expects you to know rig terminology yet. Day one is orientation: safety induction, a walkaround of the location so you know where the muster points and fire extinguishers are, PPE issue if the contractor supplies it, and introductions to the crew you'll be shadowing. For the first several shifts you're mostly watching and doing the simplest, lowest-risk tasks under direct supervision — housekeeping, moving supplies, painting, helping stage equipment — while someone more experienced explains what's happening around you and why. Nobody is evaluating your technical skill in week one, because you don't have any yet and that's expected. What they're actually watching is whether you show up on time, follow instructions without having to be told twice, and ask questions instead of guessing.</p>

      <h3>What Actually Transfers From Other Jobs</h3>
      <p>People worry they're starting from nothing, but most backgrounds carry something real into this work. Construction and warehouse work translate the most directly — you already know how to move heavy material safely, work around machinery, and take direction from a crew lead. Military service translates heavily on the discipline and safety-culture side: following a checklist exactly, understanding why a procedure exists even when it slows you down, and not cutting corners under time pressure. Farm and ranch work translates on physical endurance and comfort with long outdoor hours in bad weather. Even retail or food service carries something: the ability to stay sharp and polite through a tiring 10-to-12-hour shift is a real, transferable skill, and it's one a lot of technically-stronger candidates actually lack. None of this is a substitute for the hands-on learning that only happens on location, but it means almost nobody is starting completely from zero in every sense.</p>

      <h3>The Real Adjustment Period</h3>
      <p>The honest version: the first two to four weeks are the hardest, and it's mostly physical, not technical. Twelve-hour shifts on your feet, in whatever weather the location has that week, doing repetitive manual labor, is a real adjustment even for someone who considers themselves fit — it's a different kind of tired than a gym session or a desk job, and most new hands underestimate it until they've lived through a few tours. It gets easier specifically because your body adapts to the schedule and the work, not because the work itself gets lighter. If you're traveling to a location away from home, the isolation and the unfamiliar routine add a second adjustment on top of the physical one, which is exactly why <a href="/careers/rotation-schedules">rotation schedules</a> exist — the time off at the end of a hitch is a deliberate structural reset, not a bonus.</p>

      <h3>What Gets You Kept On — or Let Go — in the First 90 Days</h3>
      <p>Technical skill is almost never the reason someone doesn't make it past their first few months, because nobody is hired expecting technical skill yet. What actually ends a new hire's time on a rig, in roughly the order it happens: missing a shift or showing up late without a real reason, failing a drug or alcohol test, ignoring a safety instruction or cutting a corner to finish faster, and — less dramatically but just as common — being someone the crew can't rely on, whether that's complaining constantly, needing to be told the same thing repeatedly, or being visibly unwilling to do the less interesting parts of the job. On the other side, what actually gets someone promoted off the bottom rung fastest isn't raw strength or speed; it's the same thing every experienced hand on this site started out doing, covered in the <a href="/careers/roustabout#what-new-roustabouts-get-wrong">step-by-step roustabout guide</a>: show up early, ask before you assume, and treat the "boring" tasks as seriously as the dangerous ones, because on a rig floor they're often the same tasks.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Guides</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Career <span>Guides</span></h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🪜</span>
        <h3>How to Become a Roustabout</h3>
        <p>The true entry point — no experience, no certifications onshore. What the job involves and how to actually get hired.</p>
        <a href="/careers/roustabout" class="card-link">Read the Guide →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔧</span>
        <h3>How to Become a Roughneck</h3>
        <p>The complete entry-level path — no experience needed. Certifications, physical requirements, and how to actually get hired.</p>
        <a href="/careers/roughneck" class="card-link">Read the Guide →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🪢</span>
        <h3>How to Become a Derrickhand</h3>
        <p>The promotion earned, not applied for — working the monkey board and owning the mud system. What it pays and the IWCF Level 2 gate.</p>
        <a href="/careers/derrickhand" class="card-link">Read the Guide →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🎛️</span>
        <h3>How to Become a Driller</h3>
        <p>The path from floorhand to running the console. What IWCF levels you need and how long it really takes.</p>
        <a href="/careers/driller" class="card-link">Read the Guide →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔥</span>
        <h3>How to Become a Rig Welder</h3>
        <p>Welding certifications, process qualifications, and the path to underwater welding or CWI inspector.</p>
        <a href="/welding/certifications" class="card-link">Read the Guide →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">💰</span>
        <h3>Oil Rig Salary Guide</h3>
        <p>Real pay ranges for every position, onshore vs offshore, and how day rates and rotations affect take-home pay.</p>
        <a href="/careers/salary" class="card-link">See Salaries →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📜</span>
        <h3>Certifications Explained</h3>
        <p>BOSIET, IWCF, NEBOSH, H₂S — what each certification covers and which roles require them.</p>
        <a href="/careers/certifications" class="card-link">See Certifications →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🌊</span>
        <h3>Offshore vs Onshore</h3>
        <p>Pay differences, rotation schedules, lifestyle tradeoffs, and how to decide which path fits you.</p>
        <a href="/careers/offshore-vs-land-rig-jobs" class="card-link">Compare →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🪜</span>
        <h3>Rig Floor Leadership</h3>
        <p>The supervisory ladder above driller — toolpusher, OIM/rig manager, and company man — and the certification that gates each step up.</p>
        <a href="/pillars/rig-floor-leadership" class="card-link">See the Ladder →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">⚖️</span>
        <h3>IWCF vs IADC WellSharp</h3>
        <p>Which well control certification your contractor actually wants, and how the two schemes' levels map onto each other.</p>
        <a href="/careers/iwcf-vs-iadc-wellsharp" class="card-link">Compare →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Overview</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rig <span>Hierarchy</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:640px;margin-bottom:32px;">Every person on a rig has a specific function. Drilling crews operate in 8- or 12-hour tours (shifts), with clear lines of authority from floorhand to company man.</p>

    <div class="hierarchy">
      <div class="hier-row header">
        <span>Position</span><span>Responsibilities</span><span>Pay Range (US)</span><span>Experience</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name"><a href="/careers/company-man" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">Company Man</a></span><span class="role-level">Client Rep</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">On-site representative of the oil company funding operations. Final authority on well program decisions.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$150K–$300K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">15+ yrs</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name"><a href="/careers/toolpusher" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">Toolpusher</a></span><span class="role-level">Rig Supervisor</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">Overall supervisor of rig contractor's operations. Manages all crews, tools, and equipment.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$90K–$180K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">10–15 yrs</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name"><a href="/careers/driller" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">Driller</a></span><span class="role-level">Crew Supervisor</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">Controls drilling from the console. Monitors WOB, RPM, torque, pump pressure. Supervises the crew.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$70K–$120K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">5–8 yrs</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name">Derrickman</span><span class="role-level">Specialist</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">Works the monkey board 90–100 ft up guiding pipe stands. Operates mud pumps and monitors pit volumes.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$55K–$90K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">3–5 yrs</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name"><a href="/careers/roughneck" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">Roughneck</a></span><span class="role-level">Rig Floor Crew</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">Makes up/breaks out pipe connections. Operates the catwalk. The backbone of drilling operations.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$45K–$70K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">1–3 yrs</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hier-row">
        <div><span class="role-name"><a href="/careers/roustabout" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;">Roustabout</a></span><span class="role-level">Entry Level</span></div>
        <div class="role-desc">General labor: painting, cleaning, moving supplies, assisting crane operations. Entry point for most careers.</div>
        <div class="role-pay">$38K–$55K</div>
        <div class="role-exp">No exp required</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
